<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Bai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 701–<a href="/years/762">762</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Taibai, Li Po, Li Bo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Bai, also known by his courtesy name Taibai, was a Chinese poet counted among the most important of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>, whose poetry, with that of his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/du-fu">Du Fu</a>, is often called the Golden Age of Chinese Poetry. Around 1,000 poems attributed to him survive; some were collected in the Heyue yingling ji, compiled in 753 by Yin Fan, and thirty-four are in Three Hundred Tang Poems. His poems celebrate friendship, nature, solitude and drinking, and his later ones reflect the rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a>, which left most of Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> devastated by war and famine. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezra">Ezra</a> Pound's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cathay">Cathay</a> (1915) drew 11 of its 19 poems from him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-bai.jpg" alt="Li Bai" width="330" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:李白.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tang princesses became Daoist nuns, and poets such as <b>Li Bai</b> were initiated into the religion.</blockquote><a href="/religion/daoism">Daoism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tang dynasty, from the 7th to the 10th centuries, was its golden age, with poets such as <b>Li Bai</b>, Du Fu and Wang Wei; a collection of the complete poems of the Tang compiled in 1705 contains nearly fifty thousand poems by more than two thousand poets.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;So bright a gleam on the foot of my bed— Could there have been a frost already? Lifting myself to look, I found that it was moonlight. Sinking back again, I thought suddenly of home.&#8221;</p><footer>&quot;In the Quiet Night&quot; &#183; tr. Witter Bynner, 1929 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Jade_Mountain/In_the_Quiet_Night" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;From a pot of wine among the flowers I drank alone. There was no one with me— Till, raising my cup, I asked the bright moon To bring me my shadow and make us three.&#8221;</p><footer>&quot;Drinking Alone with the Moon&quot; &#183; tr. Witter Bynner, 1929 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Jade_Mountain/Drinking_Alone_with_the_Moon" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I sang. The moon encouraged me. I danced. My shadow tumbled after. As long as I knew, we were boon companions. And then I was drunk, and we lost one another.&#8221;</p><footer>&quot;Drinking Alone with the Moon&quot; &#183; tr. Witter Bynner, 1929 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Jade_Mountain/Drinking_Alone_with_the_Moon" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
